title: Unto Thee Will I Pray – Prayer, Praise and Worship
description: Prayer, praise and worship: the Psalms as a model of prayer, how God answers prayer, persistent prayer, imprecatory prayer, vows, and singing His praise, from the teachings on prayer.
output: lessons/doctrine/topic-prayer.html
highlight: pray

# One deck for the topic "Prayer, praise & worship" (67 teachings), its themes given equal weight.
# Slide text is the author's own sentences, trimmed. The verses the teachings typed out are shown from the KJV.
# Fixed: "hell and deaf" -> "hell and death" (17712), "a playful song" -> "a prayerful song" (17672),
# "walking 3the Spirit" -> "walking in the Spirit" (14704), "decency on God" -> "dependency on God" (17666).

=== Introduction
--- Unto Thee Will I Pray [title]
overline: Doctrine · Prayer, praise & worship
heading: Unto Thee Will I Pray
sub: How to pray, how God answers, vows, and praise.
refs: Psalms 5:2-3; Matthew 6:9-13; Luke 18:1; Hebrews 4:16; Ephesians 5:19-20
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

# from 9385
--- So, pray
big: Matthew 6:9 | mark: After this manner therefore pray ye
p: Jesus not only taught us the pattern of prayer, but He lived a prayerful life. Often. Earnestly. Seeking God's will and the acceptance to do it. Alone. Privately. Early in the morning. All night if needed. On your knees. In tears and joy. Praising God in thanksgiving. Seeking forgiveness. Asking for favor, always in His will.
p: Not publicly and loudly to show how holy you are. Not in vain repetition, but in humble conversation, directly with God our Father.

=== One mediator
# from 9385, 14796, 17843
--- Boldly before the throne
kicker: One mediator
big: 1 Timothy 2:5 | mark: one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus
p: We pray to the Father, in the name of the Son, through the power of the Spirit ([[Romans 8:26]]). Praying directly to God without a need for any other intermediary. Not praying to dead spirits, which is divination and necromancy. Not praying through a “mediatrix;” Jesus didn't say “Our mother...”
p: Many pray directly to angels, which is wicked ([[Revelation 22:8-9]]; [[Colossians 2:18]]). We pray, as David did, to the Living God. He directs His hosts.
p: Catholics use idols and “Mary,” and “saints” and priests as intermediaries, because they know in their hearts they cannot go to the Father as sons. Very sad. We go boldly before the Father with confidence as sons ([[Hebrews 4:16]]).

# from 4885, 4600
--- Father and friend
kicker: One mediator
big: Psalms 5:3 | mark: My voice shalt thou hear in the morning, O LORD
p: I have conversations with God all day, everyday. He points out things and I come to Him with my problems. We should be reverent and respectful but He's also our closest and dearest friend.
p: Having adult children, you notice that when your kids are up to something they know you wouldn't approve of, they go silent suddenly. That's how God sees it when we're not praying to Him everyday. Don't let sin in your life stop you from talking to God about it. Don't let embarrassment keep you from Him.
read: app:doctrine-prayer~9385 | Pray directly to the Father: one mediator
read: app:doctrine-prayer~4600 | God sees when we go quiet

=== The Psalms as a model of prayer
# from 17658, 17663
--- Lord, my God, hear me
kicker: The Psalms as a model
big: Psalms 4:8 | mark: I will both lay me down in peace, and sleep
p: We often don't know how to pray, let alone in the midst of trouble. But the Psalms are given as a foundation of prayer. “Lord, my God, hear me” is an excellent start any time you go boldly before the Throne.
p: *Stand in awe, and sin not: commune with your own heart upon your bed, and be still* ([[Psalms 4:4]]). Psalm 4 is prayer in the night before sleeping; Psalm 5, prayer to start the morning. Day and night, we should be in prayer to our Father.

# from 17666, 17871, 17875
--- Praise, humility, mercy, then judgment
kicker: The Psalms as a model
big: Psalms 41:4 | mark: heal my soul; for I have sinned against thee
p: Before anything else, the Psalmist has prioritized calling out to God, humbling himself, admitting fault and dependency on God. This should be our starting position in prayer. Praise, humility, mercy, then judgment.
p: Many of us use prayer as a means of complaining, to whine about it being unfair that we are suffering at all. The problem with this is it lacks the self-reflection and humility.
p: Watch out for the creeping pride that may come from being recognized as a “prayer warrior.” Begin with confession. A contrite heart with love unfeigned can move mountains.

# from 17668, 17784
--- Reminding God of His promises
kicker: The Psalms as a model
big: Psalms 6:4-5 | mark: oh save me for thy mercies’ sake
p: To remind God of His word shows you actually know it, and trust in it. I think this is pleasing to the Lord. Letting God know how weary it has become is also an effective means of working it through yourself.
p: *If my people... shall humble themselves, and pray, and seek my face* ([[2 Chronicles 7:14]]). When we seek the Lord, it is obedience to His command to seek His face ([[Psalms 27:8]]).

# from 14038, 14041
--- Thanksgiving, and to be used
kicker: The Psalms as a model
big: Psalms 56:11 | mark: In God have I put my trust: I will not be afraid what man can do unto me
p: I wish more of the prayer time in a service were of thanksgiving than for asking. God already knows our needs. With all sincere humility, glorify God for answered prayers. People need to hear that. It gives hope to the hopeless.
p: The most surprising thing you find when you walk in the spirit is that what you start to actually pray for, instead of your fears and needs, is that He use you. That you can be the means of blessing someone else, because you know He's already got you.
read: app:doctrine-prayer~17666 | Praise, humility, mercy, then judgment
read: app:doctrine-prayer~17875 | Begin with confession: a warning to prayer warriors
read: app:doctrine-prayer~14038 | Thanksgiving in prayer

=== How God answers prayer
# from 5414, 6302
--- Ask, believing
kicker: How God answers
big: Mark 11:24-25 | mark: believe that ye receive them, and ye shall have them; forgive, if ye have ought against any
p: This is not simply an affirmation to put on Instagram. It is a promise from God. All He asks is your forgiveness for others as He has forgiven you. To not hold grudges.
p: How can we humble ourselves to ask for forgiveness for disobeying God when we hold back forgiveness from those who've wronged us? Don't treat it lightly, but as literally true. Grow in faith, living faith to faith.

# from 14861, 15143, 13534, 13535
--- According to His will
kicker: How God answers
big: 1 John 5:14 | mark: if we ask any thing according to his will, he heareth us
p: Jabez called on the God of Israel, *And God granted him that which he requested* ([[1 Chronicles 4:10]]). It will be in His time. It may not look like what you imagined. It may be what you need not what you want.
p: When I first seek the things of God, and pray for His will to be done, I am blessed beyond measure. This isn't a prosperity gospel. Seeking His will first will find you asking for ways to please Him, to be a blessing to others, rather than riches.

# from 15956, 16218, 14704
--- Your iniquities have separated you
kicker: How God answers
big: Isaiah 59:1-2 | mark: your iniquities have separated between you and your God
p: It isn't any failure of His that we have trouble. He can still save. The fault lies with us, when our iniquity has separated us from our God ([[Ezekiel 20:3]]). Many claim to “want to know God's will for their lives,” but flee from hard preaching against their sin.
p: When we are in sin, and ashamed, our doubts and fears increase. Thankfully, if we turn to Him, and confess our sins, He will forgive us ([[1 John 1:9]]). Walk in the light that we may have confidence in the Lord to deliver us.

# from 17712, 16255
--- When the answer is no
kicker: How God answers
big: 2 Corinthians 12:8-9 | mark: My grace is sufficient for thee
p: If a prayer remains “unanswered” by our measure, it very well may be answered in His. We've already been spared, hell and death, and given the glory of eternal life in Christ.
p: When we have a decision to make, and we consult any other source but God, He may well allow bad advice that tickles your itching ear ([[Ezekiel 21:21]]). Seek ye first the Kingdom of God. Start in His word and in prayer. Content with whatever His will may be.

# from 9313, 16054
--- Prayer and fasting
kicker: How God answers
big: Matthew 17:20-21 | mark: this kind goeth not out but by prayer and fasting
p: Jesus here is telling us that it was a lack of faith, and how to rectify that is by prayer and fasting. If we encounter things we struggle to overcome, it is by prayer and fasting that we can grow in faith enough to overcome. Simple as.
p: Daniel read the word of God and, believing it to be true, began to plea for mercy, not of their righteousness, but by His mercy ([[Daniel 9:18]]). If we all held to the spirit of Daniel, and prayed like him, those things would work themselves out.
read: app:doctrine-prayer~6302 | Have faith in God and forgive when ye pray
read: app:doctrine-prayer~13535 | Seek first His will: not a prosperity gospel
read: app:doctrine-prayer~16054 | The spirit of Daniel's prayer

=== Pray, and not faint
# from 17667, 17725, 16059
--- Importunity
kicker: Pray, and not faint
big: Luke 18:1 | mark: men ought always to pray, and not to faint
p: Importunity means asking for something over and over again in a strong, pushy way. It may seem contrary to the fear of the Lord, but scripture shows it is effective. We tend to move on, rather than “bother God.” We have not because we ask not.
p: Daniel's answer was delayed 21 days, matching the three weeks he had been mourning ([[Daniel 10:12-13]]). We should not give up in prayer when we aren't seeing them answered immediately.
p: Settling it in your heart to be glad with whatever God has given you will often lead to Him blessing you with yet a better outcome. I have personally experienced this.

# from 17845
--- Don't bargain with God
kicker: Pray, and not faint
big: Psalms 35:18 | mark: I will give thee thanks in the great congregation
p: Some try to bargain with God. “I'll go to church more, I will read my Bible more.” Those are things that are the least we could do, even as unprofitable servants. We have nothing to give but our love and praise in exchange.
p: When we bargain, we give ourselves a means to boast. We begin to see our efforts as the reason God “owed it to me.” Avoid anything that shares the glory with Almighty God.
read: app:doctrine-prayer~17725 | Importunity and imprecatory prayer are Biblical
read: app:doctrine-prayer~17845 | Avoid bargaining with God, give Him the glory

=== Imprecatory prayer
# from 9629, 17725
--- Shall not God avenge his own elect?
kicker: Imprecatory prayer
big: Luke 18:7-8 | mark: shall not God avenge his own elect
p: Notice the woman before the unjust judge is pleading for vengeance against her adversary, just as David does in the psalms, just as the martyred under the throne in Heaven ([[Revelation 6:10]]).
p: Imprecatory prayers are Biblical. When Jesus teaches us to pray, He says “Thy kingdom come.” Some teachers claim there is no room for this in the New Testament. But they are a mighty tool in our arsenal of faith.

# from 17844, 17694
--- Love your enemies, yet pray for His vengeance
kicker: Imprecatory prayer
big: Psalms 35:13 | mark: when they were sick, my clothing was sackcloth
p: When they were sick, David humbled himself in prayer and fasting. He shows them love. That is what Jesus asked of us ([[Matthew 5:44]]). When they are weak, or in need, we are to love them.
p: But we have no obligation to pray they succeed in the evil they do. We are called to love our personal enemies. But those that oppress the righteous, God will judge, and we can pray that God will repay. What little we can do in our wrath? Better to rejoice in the wrath of God.

# from 17726, 17672
--- Search me first
kicker: Imprecatory prayer
big: Psalms 17:3 | mark: Thou hast proved mine heart; I am purposed that my mouth shall not transgress
p: It is foolish to cry out for God's perfect justice while you yourself are guilty. Like a thief going to the police because his partners robbed him of his share.
p: The pattern of imprecatory prayer includes self-examination and willingness for God to judge us the same way ([[Psalms 7:3-5]]). Have a clean conscience towards God. That we don't fall into a victim mentality.

# from 17788, 11808
--- Destroy the wicked, spare thine anointed
kicker: Imprecatory prayer
big: Psalms 28:9 | mark: Save thy people, and bless thine inheritance
p: We have a constant black pilling from the world. But our perspective should be to entreat the Lord, that as He punishes the wicked, He protects His people. We should feel joy rather than fear.
p: Jeremiah prayed, *forgive not their iniquity* ([[Jeremiah 18:20-23]]). We should preach the Gospel to every creature, but we shouldn't hold out hope for those who reject the truth utterly, and seek our destruction for giving it.
read: app:doctrine-prayer~17844 | Love thy enemies, and leave vengeance to the Judge
read: app:doctrine-prayer~17726 | Praying for judgment with a clean heart

=== Vows
# from 17489, 12465, 7343
--- Better not to vow
kicker: Vows
big: Ecclesiastes 5:4-5 | mark: Better is it that thou shouldest not vow, than that thou shouldest vow and not pay
p: Fools promise God a multitude of promises; turning from this sin or that, an offering for His favor, that is never paid. God takes vows seriously. He doesn't care for excuses, long public prayers, vain repetitive prayers. He wants sincere prayers and humble servants.
p: Ananias and Sapphira kept back part of the price ([[Acts 5:3-4]]). When we haven't been walking with Christ, we will make vows to God if He would provide, like Jacob ([[Genesis 28:20-22]]). God holds us to the vows we make in His name.

# from 13672
--- Jephthah's needless vow
kicker: Vows
big: Judges 11:30-31 | mark: And Jephthah vowed a vow unto the LORD
p: Jephthah would have won victory over the children of Ammon by the power of the Lord. He did not need to add his own works as part of God's plan.
p: God does not need our vows, our promises to do good, in order to deliver us. He simply asks we trust in Him and let our *yea mean yea* ([[Matthew 5:37]]). Jephthah paid a price he needn't have, because he placed a portion of his faith in his own hands.
read: app:doctrine-prayer~17489 | Better not to vow than not to pay
read: app:doctrine-prayer~13672 | Jephthah's needless vow

=== Praise and worship
# from 15096, 14943
--- The glory came with the praise
kicker: Praise and worship
big: 2 Chronicles 5:13-14 | mark: the house was filled with a cloud
p: The sacrifices and offerings were not when the cloud of God's Majesty came. It was when the praise and worship of the Lord was made. If you want to feel the power of God in your life, start with this ([[Psalms 69:30-31]]).
p: God wants us to sing songs of praise and thanksgiving, continuously. As it is in heaven, as it was in the Tabernacle and the temple. As it is in our hearts today ([[Ephesians 5:19-20]]).

# from 17827
--- Sing skilfully
kicker: Praise and worship
big: Psalms 33:2-3 | mark: play skilfully with a loud noise
p: Music is a form of learning and worship, as much as prayer, songs with the doctrine of scripture ([[Colossians 3:16]]). What it is not, is an excuse to be carnal, to satisfy the flesh with worldly beats, mimicking a rock concert.
p: Many worship services turn into a tantric trance, swaying, captivated by the beats, with songs that are thinly veiled pop love songs, with little to no doctrine. Other churches take the instruments completely out, which seems at odds with the Bible. There is a middle ground, decently and in order.

# from 12467
--- The Lord bless thee
kicker: Praise and worship
big: Numbers 6:24-26 | mark: The LORD bless thee, and keep thee
p: The purpose is to put His name upon them. That they understand where and Who the blessings are from.
p: We should do the same, as a priesthood of believers, especially with our family, but everywhere in our lives. People need to be reminded that the blessings that come to them are of God.
read: app:doctrine-prayer~15096 | God's presence came with the praise
read: app:doctrine-prayer~17827 | Sing skilfully with instruments and doctrine

=== Pray without ceasing
--- Pray Without Ceasing [title]
overline: Prayer, praise & worship
heading: Pray Without Ceasing
big: 1 Thessalonians 5:16-18 | mark: Pray without ceasing
center: We have directly from Christ the form and context, pattern and practice of how we must pray. So, pray.
